{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792744272,"nonce":"jdPxEKsjQX1wq2MbgXh4atAEs5Q=","title":"TypeScript: arrays of arrays (number[][]) on the native path","message":"`number[][]` and deeper nesting lower natively in the interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM profiles. An element that is itself an array is stored as the address of its header, so `const row = grid[i]`, `grid[i].push(x)` and `grid[i] === row` share identity, growth and writes with the stored array. SSA stores unsized-array elements this way through one `element_storage_type` used by the list emitters, array literals, element reads and element stores.\n\nFrontend: a nested element read binds its receiver to a temporary so the bounds guard and the read see one array, and an element store hoists that binding ahead of the captured target. `grid[i].push(x)` routes through the array method path.\n\nMemory: an inner array stored into an outer one is an escape, so the shared drop pass never frees it; the outer array's own storage is released at its last use. Inner arrays therefore live until process exit. Releasing owned inner arrays needs the per-element ownership proof tracked in da0dc1d. Checked with MallocScribble and with automatic release disabled; results identical.\n\nPinned cases: arrays/nested_alias, nested_literal, nested_matmul, nested_three_levels, nested_out_of_range, nested_assignment, nested_release (interpreter, baseline, llvm).\n\nNot covered, each its own issue under 6875fc2: `new Array(n).fill(x)` construction, array methods beyond push, arrays of records (interfaces and object literals), array fields on classes.\n\nParent: 6875fc2949f83d682861ecab814e0ff82c824f5990620b0d64ca0340ebb026e5. Related: e46fc04.","files":null}]}